Abstract
## 1. Introduction
Collagen, the main component of connective tissue, is the most abundant protein found in animal body - collagen represents nearly $30\%$ of total proteins in the animal body [1]. At present, there are at least 29 known types of collagen (Type I-XXIX) [2, 3]. The various types of collagen have different amino acid sequence, structure and function. Fibrillar collagens are the most abundant collagens in vertebrates, especially type I.  Type I collagen, distributed in bone, dermis, tendon, ligaments, cornea and other organs,  play a structural role by contributing to the molecular architecture, shape, and mechanical properties of tissues [4].
The use of  type I collagen in industry for biomaterials, cosmetics, pharmaceuticals and food is expanding [2, 3]. Collagen is readily available and it possesses many interesting properties, such as biocompatibility, non-antigenecity, non-toxicity. For this reason, this protein is regarded as one of the most important and useful biopolymer in biomaterial’s research [5]. The last 20 years, increased interest has emerged in the use of collagen in biomedical application. Collagen-based materials are widely used in reconstructive medicine. Various applications of collagen in tissue regeneration may include: artificial skin, bone substitutes, implants for incontinence, artificial tendons and blood vessels, corneal implants, regeneration of cartilage and of skin and organs [4, 6]. Collagen is also a very attractive substrate for food [2] and many cosmetic applications [7]. However, its applicability is severely limited due to high cost.
Type I collagen for biomedical applications is mainly isolated from animal tissues (bovine or porcine skin and bovine or equine Achilles tendons). However,  collagen derived from animal tissues, carries potential risks for viral and prion contamination. Due to outbreak of Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE) or Transmissible Spongiform Encephalopathy (TSE), in pigs and cattle, use of collagen derived products from these sources have been limited [8, 9].
Type I collagen has also been extracted from fish wastes, such as: skin, bone, fins, and scales. Several papers have focused on the characteristics and possible isolation procedures of collagen from various fish species. There are many fresh water and marine fish. Collagens of fish studied in recent years were from: Rohu (Labeo rohita) [8], Calta (Catla catla) [8], silver carp (Hypophthalmichthys molitrix) [9, 10, 11], channel catfish (Ictalurus punctaus) [12], red seabream (Pagrus major) [13],  Nile tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus) [11, 13], golden goatfish (Parupeneus Heptacanthus) [14], brown backed toadfish (Lagocephalus gloveri) [15], Pacific saury (Cololabis saira) [16], deep-sea redfish (Sebastes mentella) [17], brownbanded bamboo shark (Chiloscyllium punctatum) [18], Baltic cod (Gadus morhua) [19] and balloon fish (Diodon holocanthus) [20], silvertip shark  (Carcharhinus albimarginatus) [21], grass carp (Ctenopharyngodon idella) [11], seabass (Lates calcarifer) [22], Amur sturgeon (Acipenser schrenckii) [23, 24] and other.
Some of papers show a procedure for isolating collagen from fish scales. Fish scales are composed of collagen fibrils type I covered with calcium salts ( $16{-}59\%$ mineral content in weight) [25]. Calcium deficient hydroxyapatite forms plate like nano-crystals with thicknesses in the range $1.5{\cdot}4~\mathrm{nm}$ [26].
Fish source is thought to be safer and attractive alternate for isolation collagen. However, fish collagen is different from mammalian collagen in its physical and chemical properties. Fish live in water and their body temperature is much lower than that of a warmblooded animal.  In particular collagens from other sources have different thermal stability and it has direct correlation with relative abundance of imino acids (hydroxyproline and proline) in collagen. The denaturation temperatures of collagens increase with their imino acid content.  The denaturation temperatures of collagen from cold-water fish are lower than those from warm-water fish. It is correlated with a lower content of imino acid (hydroxyproline and proline) compared to those of warm-water fish [8].
Surface properties of new materials are very important if we wish to use them for future biomedical applications, because a biocompatibility of biomaterials depends on the surface properties. Thus, there is a need to investigate their surface roughness, wettability and polarity. These parameters are important factor for cell-material interaction [27].
The objective of this investigation was to isolate and characterise acid soluble and pepsin soluble fish collagen from new source. Collagen used for our study was isolated from scales of fresh water fish, northern pike (Esox lucius).  Esox lucius is a member of Esocidae family. This fish is found in fresh water of temperate countries in Europe [28]. This fish, naturally inhabiting freshwaters in Poland, has been introduced around the world for aquaculture. Several papers devoted to possible application of fish collagen in medicine [8, 29-32], however none of them concern northern pike collagen. The isolated and purified collagen was characterized by IR, UV-Vis, SDS-PAGE analysis, amino acid analysis and DSC analysis. Denaturation temperature of the fish collagen was estimated by thermally induced change in viscosity.  The purpose of this study was also to determine properties of fish collagen films, such as: surface properties and mechanical properties.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Raw materials
Scales of Esox lucius with average body weight of $1000{-}2000\ \mathrm{g}$ were collected freshly from a fisherman (Dejguny Lake, Poland). They are caught in the age from 3 to 5 years old. The scales were removed manually and washed with chilled tap water to get rid of the adhering tissues. The scales were then packed in polyethylene bags and kept at ${\bf-}20\ ^{\circ}{\bf C}$ until used with the storage time not longer than 1 month.
## 2.2. Chemical reagents
2-Mercaptoethanol, pepsin from porcine gastric mucosa (powder, 1200-2400 U/mg), tris (hydroxylmethyl) aminomethane, sodium dodecyl sulphate (SDS), $\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{\prime}$ -methylenebis (acrylamide) and acrylamide were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Sp. z o. o. (Poznan, Poland). Protein Marker III (6.5-200) and N,N,N’,N’-tetramethylethylenediamine (TEMED) were obtained from AppliChem (Darmstadt, Germany). Acetic acid, silver nitrate, glycerol, methanol, ethanol, sodium hydroxide, sodium chloride, hydrochloric acid, bromophenol blue, sodium thiosulfate, formaldehyde, disodium ethylenediaminetetraacetate dihydrate (EDTA) and sodium carbonate were obtained from Avantor Performance Materials Poland S. A. (Gliwice, Poland). All reagents were of analytical grade.
## 2.3. Preparation of collagen
The method used for the isolation and purification of acid soluble collagen and pepsin soluble collagen is according to Matmaroh et al. [14], with some modifications. All processes were carried out at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3.1. Extraction of acid soluble collagen (ASC)
Fish scales were suspended in $0.1\mathrm{~M~NaOH}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at the ratio of 1:10 (w/v) to remove noncollagenous proteins and scales’ pigment. The solution was changed every $8\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then scales were washed with deionised water to achieve the neutral pH. Demineralisation of fish scales was carried out using $0.5{\bf M}$ EDTA solution $\mathrm{(pH=}7.4)\$ ) for $96\mathrm{~h~}$ at the ratio of 1:10 (w/v) and the solution was changed every $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The demineralised fish scale was washed thrice with deionized water. Subsequently fish scale was analyzed by energy dispersive X-ray analysis (EDX) to confirm the complete dissolution of calcium phosphate.
Then, demineralised fish scales were subjected to ASC extraction with $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ acetic acid for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ at the ratio of 1:2.5 (w/v). The mixture was filtered through two layers of cheese cloth. The supernatant was subjected to salting out by adding NaCl to obtain the concentration of $2.5~\mathrm{M}$ . The precipitated matter was collected by centrifugation at $13000\mathrm{g}$ for $^{0,5\mathrm{~h~}}$ The pellet was dissolved in $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ acetic acid and subsequently dialysed in $0.1\textbf{M}$ acetic acid, followed by distilled water for $24\mathrm{h}$ each and freeze-dried.
The completely frozen mixtures were lyophilized at ${\bf-}55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\mathrm{{Pa}}$ for 48 h (ALPHA 1-2 LD plus, CHRIST, Germany). The collagen was stored at $\mathbf{-}18\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until use. The collagen obtained was referred to as ‘acid soluble collagen, ASC’.
## 2.3.2. Extraction of pepsin soluble collagen (PSC)
Undissolved matter from acid soluble collagen extraction was further extracted in 0.5 M acetic acid containing $1\%$ pepsin (w/w) at a ratio of 1:10 (w/v) for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After extraction, the supernatant was obtained in the same method for ASC preparation. The precipitation, dialysis as well as lyophilisation were carried out in the same manner with ASC. The collagen obtained with the aid of pepsin was referred to as ‘pepsin soluble collagen, PSC’.
Both collagens were subjected to analyses.
## 2.4. Characterization of the extracted collagen
2.4.1. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) with X-ray microanalysis (EDX)
The chemical composition of demineralised fish scale was studied using Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM, LEO Electron Microscopy Ltd, England) equipped with energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX, Quantax 200, XFlash 4010, Bruker AXS, Germany).
## 2.4.2. Infrared Spectroscopy (IR)
ASC and PSC films were obtained by casting collagen solution onto $\mathrm{CaF}_{2}$ spectrophotometric windows. After slow solvent evaporation in air at room temperature thin collagen films were obtained. The IR spectra of films were obtained using a Mattson Genesis II (USA). Spectra were recorded in absorption mode at $4~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ intervals and 64 scans.
## 2.4.3. UV-VIS
The UV–VIS absorption spectra of collagen was recorded with a Shimadzu spectrophotometer (Model UV-1800). The acid- and pepsin soluble collagen samples was prepared by dissolution in $0.1\mathrm{MCH_{3}C O O H}$ .
## 2.4.4. Amino acid analysis
The amino acid composition of the Esox lucius scales collagen was analyzed at BioCentrum Ltd. (Krakow, Poland). The protein samples were hydrolyzed in the gaseous phase with 6 M HCl at $115~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The unhydrolyzed residue was also analyzed for the presence of free amino acids. The liberated amino acids were converted into phenylthiocarbamyl derivatives and analyzed by HPLC on a PicoTag $3.9\mathrm{~x~}150\mathrm{~mm}$ column (Waters, Milford, MA, USA).
## 2.4.5. Denaturation temperature
The denaturation of collagen in solution was determined from temperature induced viscosity change using an Ubbelohde viscometer, according to the method of  Pati et al. [8] with slight modification.  The viscometer was filled with $0.15~\%$ (w/v) of collagen in $0.1\textbf{M}$ $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ and incubated at $16~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then the temperature was raised stepwise to $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at intervals of $2~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and incubated for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ at each temperature. Fractional viscosity was calculated for each temperature as follows:
Fractional viscosit $\mathrm{\Lambda/=(\upeta_{\mathrm{sp(measured)}}-\upeta_{\mathrm{sp(minimum)}})/(\upeta_{\mathrm{sp(maximum)}}-\upeta_{\mathrm{sp(minimum)}})}$ where, the viscosity was specific viscosity $(\upeta_{\mathrm{sp}})$ and was calculated by following equation:
$$
\eta_{\mathrm{sp}}{=}(\mathrm{t}{-}\mathrm{t}_{0})/\mathrm{t}_{0}
$$
(t and $\mathbf{t}_{0}$ are the efflux times of collagen solution and solvent, respectively).
## 2.4.6. SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE)
SDS-PAGE was carried out using the Hoefer SE600 system. The collagen samples were dissolved in 0.1 acetic acid. The resolving gels were prepared according to the method described by Laemmli [33].  Then the samples were mixed with the sample buffer $(0.5~\mathrm{{M}}$ Tris-HCl, $\mathrm{pH}{=}6.8$ , containing $5\%$ SDS, $20\%$ glycerol) at the ratio 1:2 in the presence of $10\%$ 2-mercaptoethanol. Electrophoresis was performed using a $7.5\%$ resolving gel and $4\%$ stacking gel. After electrophoresis proteins were visualized by silver staining as described by Merril [34]. $5\upmu\mathrm{g}$ of protein was loaded in each well.
## 2.4.7. Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC)
Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) measurements were performed using the DSC 204 F1 Phoenix (NETZSCH-Gerätebau GmbH) at a rate of $10~\mathrm{{^{\circ}C/m i n}}$ from 20 to 250 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . DSC curves for each film were obtained from the first heating run. The weight of each sample film was about ${5{-}}6~\mathrm{{mg}}$ . Aluminium crucibles were used to obtain the DSC curves. The exothermic peaks of the thermogram were monitored.
## 2.4.8. Mechanical properties
Acid- and pepsin soluble collagen films were obtained by casting collagen solution onto glass plate. After slow solvent evaporation in air at room temperature thin collagen films were obtained. Mechanical properties of ASC and PSC collagen films have been measured by Zwick&Roell $Z0.5$ testing machine. These samples were stretched with the speed $10\mathrm{mm/min}$ in air. Five samples of each type were tested. The data were presented as means $\pm$ standard deviation (SD).
## 2.4.9. Contact angle measurements
Contact angles of two liquids: diiodomethane and glycerol on collagen films were measured at constant room temperature using goniometer equipped with a system of dropshaped analysis (DSA 10 produced by Krüss, Germany). The surface free energy was calculated using Owens-Wendt method.
## 2.4.10. Atomic force microscopy
The surface properties of ASC and PSC films were observed using atomic force microscope. Topographic imaging were performed in air using a commercial AFM a MultiMode Scanning Probe Microscope Nanoscope IIIa (Digital Instruments Veeco Metrology Group, Santa Barbara, CA) operating in the tapping mode in air.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Energy dispersive X-ray analysis (EDX)
The scales of teleost fish are composed of calcium - deficient hydroxyapatite and collagen fibers. Thus in order to get the collagen, the fish scales first should be demineralized. Demineralization of Esox lucius scales was completed after $96\mathrm{~h~}$ of EDTA treatment, which was confirmed by EDX (Table 1).
## 3.2. Infrared Spectroscopy (IR)
IR spectra of acid soluble and pepsin soluble collagen of northern pike are shown in Fig.1. The major peaks in the spectra both ASC and PSC from Esox lucius were similar (Table 2). We observe the bands typical for collagen such N-H stretching  at ${\sim}3312~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for the amide A, C-H stretching at ${\sim}3076~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for the amide B, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching at $\sim1653~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for the amide I  and N-H deformation at $\sim1548~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for the amide II. The spectra of the ASC and PSC from Esox lucius also demonstrated the characteristic amide III band at $1235{\cdot}1237\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ resulting from $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=O}$ stretching.  It is similar to that other species collagens [12, 17, 35].
## 3.3. UV-VIS
UV-Vis spectra of acid soluble and pepsin soluble collagen isolated from Esox lucius scales are presented in Fig. 2.  Generally, the maximum absorption wavelength of protein in the near ultraviolet region is $280\mathrm{nm}$ . The aromatic side chains of tyrosine and phenylalanine contained in a collagen absorb light in the $240{-}300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ region (absorption maximum for tyrosine is $275\ \mathrm{nm}$ and  for phenylalanine is $258~\mathrm{nm},$ [36]. Due to the low content of tyrosine and phenylalanine no evident absorption in this region was observed. As can be seen from the UV-Vis spectra, both ASC and PSC have absorption in the region 210-240 nm which is similar to those of collagens from channel catfish [12] and balloon fish [20].
## 3.4. Amino acid analysis
The amino acid composition of Esox lucius acid soluble and pepsin soluble collagen is shown in Table 3. The collagen triple-helix is characterized by a distinctive repeating sequence: (Gly-X-Y)n, where Gly is glycine, X is a variable and frequently is proline (Pro) and $\mathrm{Y}$ is variable and frequently is hydroxyproline (Hyp) [37]. Amino acid analysis results indicate, that glycine is the most abundant amino acid  in northern pike collagen and constitute approximately one third of total amino acid with abundant 351-363 residues/1000 residues. Both ASC and PSC are rich in alanine (120-122 residues/1000 residues), glutamine/glutamic acid (67 residues/1000 residues), arginine (45-50 residues/1000 residues) and asparagine/aspartic acid (40-41 residues/1000 residues). Very low contents of tyrosine (3-4 residues/1000 residues) was observed.
For the structural integrity of collagen the amount of imino acid (proline and hydroxyproline) is important.  The total imino acid content of northern pike was about $19\%$ . It was more than some reports from collagen from cold water fish species (channel catfish ${\sim}17\%$ [12], deep-sea redfish ${\sim}16\%$ [17]). However, this is significantly lower than those of collagens from tropical fish species [8]. The total content of imino acid in Esox lucius collagen is similar to  the content of Hyp and Pro in silver carp collagen [10]. Both species live in alike climate. It is known that different hydroxyproline contents between species might depend upon the species, environment and body temperature of fish [8, 13]. Furthermore, it was reported that imino acid content is also directly correlated with thermal stability of collagen - the denaturation temperatures of collagens increase with their imino acid content [3, 10, 31].
## 3.5. Denaturation temperature
The denaturation temperature $\mathrm{(T_{d})}$ was determined by viscosity measurement. Relative viscosity of acid soluble and pepsin soluble Esox lucius collagen in $0.1\textbf{M}$ acetic acid is depicted in Fig. 3. $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of collagen from the scale of Esox lucius was found to be $28.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for ASC and $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for PSC.  Denaturation temperature of ASC was relatively higher than that of PSC. This value is usually characteristic for collagen from fresh-water fish and some marine species. Thermal stability investigated by viscosity measurements was similar to that of silver carp ( $28~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC, skin) [9], balloon fish ( $29.01\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC, skin; $30.01\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - PSC, skin) [20], brown backed toadfish $28~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ PSC, skin) [15], Pagrus major ( $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - PSC, scales) [13], tigger puffer $28.4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC, skin) [38] and slightly higher than that of Pacific saury collagen ( $24{\cdot}25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - PSC, scales) [16].
$\mathrm{T_{d}}$ values of ASC and PSC from Esox lucius were much lower than that of mammalian collagen, e. g. calf skin collagen $36.3~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , ASC) [39] and rat tail tendon collagen $(39\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC) [40]. Moreover, $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ values of Esox lucius collagen was also lower than that of warmwater fish, such as Calta and Rohu ( ${\sim}35.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC, scale) [8], black drum $34.2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC; $35.8~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - PSC, skin) and sheepshead ( $34.0~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC; $34.3~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - PSC, skin )  [41]. However, compared with the collagens from some marine species, the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of collagen from Esox lucius scales is much higher than those of collagens from Alaska Pollack skin ( $16.8\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , skin - ASC; $18.4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - swim bladder, ASC) [42], chub salmon ( $19.4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , muscle) [43], Baltic cod ( $14.4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC, backbone) [19], deep-sea redfish ( $16.1~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC, skin, $17.7\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ - ASC, scale) [17].
Generally, $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of collagen from warm-water fish is higher than that of cold-water fish due to lower content of hydroxyproline in collagen obtained from fish species living in cold environments in comparison with content of Hyp in collagen isolated from fish living in warm environments.
## 3.6. SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE)
The acid soluble and pepsin soluble collagen from Esox lucius were examined by SDS-PAGE using a $7.5\%$ resolving gel. SDS-PAGE patterns of the isolated collagens are shown in Fig. 4. The protein patterns for both ASC and PSC were quite similar. ASC and PSC collagen from Esox lucius scales had two differential $\mathfrak{a}$ chains ( $\mathbf{\bar{\alpha}}(\mathbf{\alpha}_{\mathrm{{a}_{1}}}$ and ${\bf a}_{2}$ ) as a major constituents. In both ASC and PSC, the molecular mass of collagen was about $118\mathrm{{kDa}}$ for ${\bf a}_{1}$ and $108~\mathrm{{kDa}}$ for ${\bf q}_{2}$ .  High molecular weight components, including $\upbeta$ (dimmer) and $\gamma$ (trimmer)  - components were also observed in both ASC and PSC. The obtained protein patterns and observation were similar to reported results for collagen from a lot of other fish species [7, 11, 12, 34]. It suggests that collagen isolated from Esox lucius scales was probably type I collagen.
In the case of ${\bf a}_{1}$ and ${\bf{a}}_{2}$ chains in the PSC we observed slightly lower molecular weight, compared with those of ASC. Similar results were obtained by Matmaroh et al. [14]. Moreover, the band intensity of $\upbeta$ and $\gamma$ chains in ASC was visibly higher compared with the PSC. Those differences may results from the influence of pepsin, which has the ability to chain cleavage fragments located in the telopeptide region. As a result, a part of the peptide is removed, which in turn results in less molecular weight of the protein chain.
## 3.7. Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC)
DSC thermograms of acid soluble and pepsin soluble collagen samples from the northern pike are shown in Fig. 5. As can be seen the thermograms for all samples indicated two different endothermal peaks. The first peak is related to temperature of thermal denaturation of collagen and the water bonded to molecules. The second endothermic peak illustrate the changes in structure of cross-linked part of collagen leading to complete destruction of this protein. $\mathrm{T}_{1}$ for ASC $(79.3~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and PSC  ( $80.1~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ are very similar.  The maximum of peak for collagen at ${\sim}79~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is comparable to Arius parkeri $\mathrm{T}_{1}{=}70.9~^{\circ}C\mathrm{)}$ and Cynosian leiarchus $(\mathrm{T}_{1}{=}74.9\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ [44]. However, the obtained results are not compared with the results of DSC study reported in literature for other collagens from fish scales [14]. Varying thermal transition or temperature denaturation depend on method of collagen extraction, imino acid and water contents and habitat temperature, where the fish live.
We observed also that $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ is slightly higher for PSC $(203.1~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ than for acid soluble collagen $(198.4^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ).
## 3.8. Mechanical properties
Mechanical properties of materials have been measured only for acid soluble collagen film. Films obtained by PSC casting solution and solvent evaporation were very brittle and it wasn’t possible to cut out shapes for measurement of mechanical properties.
Young’s Modulus (E), ultimate tensile strength $(\delta_{\mathrm{M}})$ and ultimate percentage of elongation $\bf(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{{M}}})$ of acid soluble collagen film has been shown in Table 4. The results of mechanical properties measurements confirm that the Young’s modulus of collagen from scales of northern pike is similar to Young’s modulus of collagen isolated from rat tail tendon $(\mathrm{E}{\sim}1.3\mathrm{GPa})$ ) [45].
The results suggest that  the film-forming ability of ASC prepared from the scales of Esox lucius was better than PSC.  Moreover, many methods to improve the physical properties (such as mechanical and thermal properties) of collagen films are currently known - a variety of crosslinking methods have been described, including e. g. UV irradiation, heat, carbodiimide, transglutaminase [46]. These methods can be used in efforts to improve mechanical properties of ASC and PSC films in the future.
## 3.9. Contact angle measurements
By using diiodomethane and glycerol, hydrophilic properties of the ASC and PSC films can be studied.  The surface properties of the thin collagen films were tested by the contact angle measurements. Contact angle is a measure of non-convalent forces between liquid and surface of material. The results of contact angle measurements was used to determine the surface free energy $[\gamma_{\mathrm{s}}]$ .  The values of surface free energy, dispersive $[\gamma_{\bf s}^{\mathrm{{d}}}]$ and polar $[\gamma_{\bf s}^{\mathrm{\p}}]$ components of surface free energy of acid soluble and pepsin soluble fish collagen films have been presented in Table 6.
The value of surface free energy for ASC film is higher than for PSC film. It is worth noting that the polar component of surface free energy $[\gamma_{\bf s}^{\mathrm{\p}}]$ has much lower value for PSC $(\gamma_{\mathrm{{s}}}^{\mathrm{{p}}}\mathrm{{=}}6.8)$ than that for ASC collagen film $\langle\gamma_{\mathrm{s}}^{\mathrm{\p}}=13.5\rangle$ . The results prove that surface of ASC collagen film is more polar than PSC. It could be caused large content of polar terminal group in the surface of the first one. Bigger surface free energy may lead to the improvement of adhesion ability of the material [27]. The differences between values of dispersive components of surface free energy for ASC and PSC are not significant $(\gamma_{\mathrm{s}}^{\mathrm{~d~}}=25.2$ and 26.2 for ASC and PSC, respectively).
## 3.10. Atomic force microscopy
Surface topography play a crucial role in the bioactivity of biomaterials. Therefore, it is important to determine the surface roughness of materials. We compare the surface roughness of acid soluble and pepsin soluble collagen films (Fig. 6). The average roughness $\mathrm{[R_{a}]}$ and root mean square roughness $[\mathrm{R_{q}}]$ obtained by AFM was analyzed. The $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{q}}$ value  has been shown in Table 6. The mean roughness of the films fabricated with acid soluble collagen was $5.4\ \mathrm{nm\(R_{a})}$ and 6.8 $(\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{q}})$ nm. AFM technique allows also to observe the fibrils. Banded collagen fibrils can be seen in the dried ASC films (Fig. 6a and Fig. 6b). Diameter of these fibers is $67\mathrm{nm}$ (Table 6).
Whereas in the case when the collagen was isolated from fish scales with pepsin addition we observed significant changes in surface roughness of collagen film.  As can be seen, the films obtained with pepsin soluble collagen were rougher: $\mathrm{R_{a}}{=}14~\mathrm{nm}$ and $\mathrm{R_{q}}{=}17~\mathrm{nm}$ . For the dried pepsin soluble collagen films  no distinct  fibrils and bundles were observed, as shown on Fig. 6c and Fig. 6d. Telopeptide regions are important for stabilizing the collagen structure. PSC was prepared  by pepsin digestion and removal of nonhelical terminal regions. It suggests that due to lack of telopeptides PSC is not supposed to organize in fibrils, whereas ASC has ability to form fibrils.
## 4. Conclusions
Collagen type I was successfully isolated from the scales of Esox lucius by two methods:  using acetic acid or acetic acid with addition of pepsin. This collagen has a distinct amino acid composition and a denaturation temperature different from those of mammalian collagens. Denaturation temperatures of Esox lucius collagen was also lower than that of warm-water fish. However, compared with the collagens from some marine species, the $\mathrm{T_{d}}$ of this collagen is much higher. ASC film was more polar and less rough than PSC and we can observe the formation of collagen fibrils after solvent evaporation. ASC films had much better and comparable to rat tail tendon collagen mechanical properties. Obtained results suggest that collagen isolated from scales of Esox lucius  could be obtained effectively from the processing waste of fish, and has potential in commercial applications. However, northern pike collagen need further modification or purification in some fields.
